{
  "outcome": "partial",
  "claims": [
    {
      "part": "1. What is the effect of the registration of a caveat?",
      "text": "The registration of a caveat requires that the caveator be served with a notice of the presentation for registration of any instrument affecting the land in respect of which the caveat is registered.",
      "citations": ["SRC005:s32", "SRC009:s32"]
    },
    {
      "part": "2. Can Romani file a caveat in respect of her property? Who can file a caveat under the Registration of Documents Ordinance, as amended?",
      "text": "Yes, Romani can file a caveat in respect of her property because she has a right, title, or interest in it. Under the Ordinance, a caveat may be filed by any person (or body of persons) who has or claims to have a right, title, or interest to a land, an undivided land, a land parcel, or a condominium parcel. This specifically includes a beneficiary under a trust, a lawful guardian or next friend of a minor or person of unsound mind or mental deficiency, an attorney, a judgment creditor, an executor or administrator of an estate, a legal heir, or an intended purchaser who has entered into an agreement with the owner or developer.",
      "citations": ["SRC005:s32", "SRC009:s32"]
    },
    {
      "part": "3. What documents must be submitted to the Land Registry when registering a caveat?",
      "text": "To register a caveat, the following must be submitted: (1) the caveat in duplicate, substantially in Form VIII of the Second Schedule; (2) an affidavit of the caveator; (3) a certificate issued by an Attorney-at-law substantiating the caveator's right, title, or interest (or claim thereto); (4) if the caveator is not the owner, copies of the notice in Form X of the Second Schedule along with stamped envelopes addressed to the owners registered in the relevant volume and folio; and (5) if the caveator is an attorney, a certified copy of the Power of Attorney registered with the Registrar General, Land Registry, or Registrar of Title.",
      "citations": ["SRC005:s32", "SRC009:s32"]
    },
    {
      "part": "4. Who is a caveator?",
      "text": "A caveator is defined as a person (including a body of persons) who has or claims to have a right, title, or interest to a land, an undivided land, a land parcel, or a condominium parcel, and presents a caveat for registration. The category includes trust beneficiaries, guardians or next friends of minors/disabled persons, attorneys, judgment creditors, executors or administrators of deceased estates, legal heirs, or intended purchasers holding agreements with owners or developers.",
      "citations": ["SRC005:s32", "SRC009:s32"]
    },
    {
      "part": "5. What is the maximum period for which a caveat remains in force?",
      "text": "The maximum period for which a caveat remains in force is not provided in the supplied statutory text.",
      "citations": ["SRC005:s32"]
    },
    {
      "part": "6. While the caveat is in force, Romani is notified by the Registrar of Lands that a document affecting the land has been presented for registration. Within how many days must Romani commence an action?",
      "text": "The timeline of days within which a caveator must commence an action after being notified by the Registrar of Lands is not provided in the supplied statutory text.",
      "citations": ["SRC005:s32"]
    }
  ],
